{
  "dom_id": "step-q133655",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following expressions are **rational**?\n\nI. ${8}^{9}+{9}^{8}$\n\nII. ${5}^{10}+\\frac{1}{200}$\n\nIII. ${2}^{8}+\\sqrt{3}$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that: \n\n- the sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Expression I is rational. Since both ${8}^{9}$ and ${9}^{8}$ are rational, their sum must be rational.\n\n- Expression II is rational. Since both ${5}^{10}$ and $\\frac{1}{200}$ are rational, their sum must be rational.\n\n- Expression III is not rational. Since ${2}^{8}$ is rational and $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational, their sum must be irrational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
